*Notes on China=E2=80=99s AI ecosystem from Stanford=E2=80=99s AI Index (20=
25)*

Let=E2=80=99s switch it up this week. In place of a translation, I want to =
take
some notes on *Stanford=E2=80=99s AI Index Report
<https://substack.com/redirect/42245506-3da9-4ea7-9fa8-ec9bfea0e40b?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*,
which was published earlier this year in April. Released annually since
2017, its evolved into an authoritative resource on AI trends. I=E2=80=99ve=
 used it
in my own research. One of my favorite stats to cite is that the U.S. leads
the world in the number of =E2=80=9Chybrid=E2=80=9D academic-corporate AI p=
ublications (at
least one co-author from industry and one co-author from academia).=C2=B9 F=
rom
this year=E2=80=99s report, I learned a lot from the section on notable mod=
els, and
where they were developed: =E2=80=9CIn 2024, the United States led with 40 =
notable
AI models, followed by China with 15 and France with three=E2=80=9D (p. 46)=
.

After slowly digesting this 457-page report, my main critique is that =E2=
=80=94 at
least, regarding the China-relevant portions =E2=80=94 the effort suffers f=
rom too
much data and too little context. Let=E2=80=99s take a closer read of three
sections:

*First, according to the index, China leads the world in AI-driven clinical
trials *(number of clinical trials that mention AI, p. 317), *but this is a
very unhelpful indicator. *

   -

   To start, the report doesn=E2=80=99t specify where they get their data o=
n
   AI-related clinical trials by region, but it=E2=80=99s very likely they =
used the
   ClinicalTrials.gov (when I searched for mentions of =E2=80=9Cartificial
   intelligence=E2=80=9D and sorted by region, the numbers were consistent =
with those
   reported by the index).
   -

   The problem is that there are significant gaps in geographical coverage
   for ClinicalTrials.gov data. For Chinese clinical trials in 2023, per *o=
ne
   study
   <https://substack.com/redirect/065122a2-bde9-452c-889a-302789baad85?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*,
   only 1,817 out of 7,658 studies were registered on ClinicalTrials.gov.
   -

   Furthermore, clinical trials measures do not reflect effective use of AI
   in healthcare. One blog about transparency in medicine *points out
   <https://substack.com/redirect/e67692c4-15c5-4753-b411-baaebfadf1e9?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*:
   =E2=80=9C*A larger number of trials does not equal more gain in useful
   scientific knowledge. For example, the UK excelled at generating robust =
and
   useful evidence on potential Covid treatments precisely because it focus=
ed
   its efforts on a small number of large, well-designed trials*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   Equipped with better background knowledge about China=E2=80=99s slow int=
egration
   of medical AI (see, for example, *ChinAI #162
   <https://substack.com/redirect/0429c501-923b-4afe-b34a-f736b4d165b4?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*),
   we should be skeptical of giving too much weight to this =E2=80=9CChina =
leads the
   world in AI-driven clinical trials=E2=80=9D indicator. In that ChinAI tr=
anslation,
   Leiphone finds that China=E2=80=99s tech giants have struggled to get ap=
provals for
   their medical AI products. As of 2021, only 15 AI products had successfu=
lly
   passed the certification process from China=E2=80=99s National Medical P=
roduct
   Administration. Now, with this context, we can leverage the AI index=E2=
=80=99s data
   to present a very different picture: by comparison, the U.S. FDA approve=
d
   129 AI medical devices in 2021 (p. 309).

*Second, the index repeats the flawed narrative of an AI optimism gap
between Asian and Western countries: =E2=80=9C**A large majority of people =
believe
AI-powered products and services offer more benefits than drawbacks in
countries like China (83%), Indonesia (80%), and Thailand (77%), while only
a minority share this view in Canada (40%), the United States (39%), and
the Netherlands (36%).**=E2=80=9D*

   -

   I=E2=80=99ve covered this in previous issues (*ChinAI #331
   <https://substack.com/redirect/ff27b873-d01c-43f4-aa56-8b26b367b0c1?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*),
   so I won=E2=80=99t belabor the point here. I do want to reiterate my ske=
pticism
   about using online samples of Chinese respondents as representative of t=
he
   overall population. In that post, I argued: =E2=80=9C*To me, here=E2=80=
=99s the bottom
   line. Regarding findings from online surveys=E2=80=A6they reflect the vi=
ews of
   highly educated adults in China, very likely concentrated in the coastal
   provinces =E2=80=94 not the general Chinese public*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   As evidence of this AI optimism gap, the index cites Ipsos online
   surveys, but note what the Ipsos survey emphasizes in its *methodology
   section
   <https://substack.com/redirect/d5e63dae-8fd8-43eb-b4ac-469baef96fc5?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*:
   =E2=80=9C*Samples in Brazil, Chile, China=E2=80=A6 are more urban, more =
educated, and/or
   more affluent than the general population. The survey results for these
   countries should be viewed as reflecting the views of the more =E2=80=9C=
connected=E2=80=9D
   segment of their population*.=E2=80=9D These are not apples-to-apples
   comparisons.

*Third, the GitHub indicators cited in the index severely understate
China=E2=80=99s investment in open-source AI software. *The Stanford report=
=E2=80=99s graph
of GitHub AI projects by geographic area (p. 78) shows a decline in
open-source AI projects from China-based developers (figure below).
Crucially, the report maps AI projects to geographic areas using *IP
address geolocation*.
<https://substack.com/redirect/7c6170b6-6189-4ae9-9922-045acfeb205f?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
Source:
Stanford AI Index 2025

   -

   Again, context matters! In his *amazing paper
   <https://substack.com/redirect/5424dbb7-cf72-4238-8b42-e9ffdb784395?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
   on open-source software policy, Jeff Gortmaker shows that IP address-bas=
ed
   measures cannot capture the activities of many Chinese developers, who f=
ace
   government restrictions on accessing the GitHub platform. Specifically,
   when analyzing Chinese contributors to open-source web development
   frameworks, he found: =E2=80=9C*By 2023, a 70,000- contributor gap betwe=
en
   China=E2=80=99s self-reported and IP-based lines likely reflects increas=
ed use of
   virtual private networks (VPNs), which, when used to bypass the GFW (Gre=
at
   Firewall), register as foreign IP addresses*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   Chip Huyen provides a more accurate assessment of global contributions
   to open-source AI software. She collected 900 of the most popular
   open-source AI tools. Among the top 20 accounts on GitHub (in terms of
   stars), 6 originated in China. That=E2=80=99s a stark contrast to the in=
dex=E2=80=99s
   metric of just 2% of GitHub AI projects coming from China.

Look, I don=E2=80=99t want to be overly harsh here. There is a lot of great
material in this 450-pg. report. These large-scale efforts are a tough
balancing act, and one could argue that the objective is to start
conversations like this one.

Still, the danger is that most people reading and aggregating these metrics
will accept them without a critical eye. I wonder if a 200-pg. report with
half as much data and two times as much expertise/context/point-of-view
would be more effective.
