1em 0 0.625em 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:1.16em;font-size:1.625em"><=
strong>Notes on China=E2=80=99s AI ecosystem from Stanford=E2=80=99s AI Ind=
ex (2025)</strong></h2><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);li=
ne-height:26px;font-size:16px"><span>Let=E2=80=99s switch it up this week. =
In place of a translation, I want to take some notes on </span><strong><a h=
ref=3D"https://substack.com/redirect/42245506-3da9-4ea7-9fa8-ec9bfea0e40b?j=
=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E" rel=3D""=
 style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underline" target=3D"_blank">=
Stanford=E2=80=99s AI Index Report</a></strong><span>, which was published =
earlier this year in April. Released annually since 2017, its evolved into =
an authoritative resource on AI trends. I=E2=80=99ve used it in my own rese=
arch. One of my favorite stats to cite is that the U.S. leads the world in =
the number of =E2=80=9Chybrid=E2=80=9D academic-corporate AI publications (=
at least one co-author from industry and one co-author from academia).</spa=
n><span style=3D"font-size:20px">=C2=B9</span><span> From this year=E2=80=
=99s report, I learned a lot from the section on notable models, and where =
they were developed: =E2=80=9CIn 2024, the United States led with 40 notabl=
e AI models, followed by China with 15 and France with three=E2=80=9D (p. 4=
6).</span></p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height=
:26px;font-size:16px">After slowly digesting this 457-page report, my main =
critique is that =E2=80=94 at least, regarding the China-relevant portions =
=E2=80=94 the effort suffers from too much data and too little context. Let=
=E2=80=99s take a closer read of three sections:</p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 =
20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><strong>First, =
according to the index, China leads the world in AI-driven clinical trials =
</strong><span>(number of clinical trials that mention AI, p. 317), </span>=
<strong>but this is a very unhelpful indicator. </strong></p><ul style=3D"m=
argin-top:0;padding:0"><li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:=
rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;margin-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;paddin=
g-left:4px;font-size:16px;margin:0">To start, the report doesn=E2=80=99t sp=
ecify where they get their data on AI-related clinical trials by region, bu=
t it=E2=80=99s very likely they used the ClinicalTrials.gov (when I searche=
d for mentions of =E2=80=9Cartificial intelligence=E2=80=9D and sorted by r=
egion, the numbers were consistent with those reported by the index). </p><=
/li><li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);line-=
height:26px;margin-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;padding-left:4px;font-siz=
e:16px;margin:0"><span>The problem is that there are significant gaps in ge=
ographical coverage for ClinicalTrials.gov data. For Chinese clinical trial=
s in 2023, per </span><strong><a href=3D"https://substack.com/redirect/0651=
22a2-bde9-452c-889a-302789baad85?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzS=
rs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E" rel=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decora=
tion:underline" target=3D"_blank">one study</a></strong><span>, only 1,817 =
out of 7,658 studies were registered on ClinicalTrials.gov.</span></p></li>=
<li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);line-heig=
ht:26px;margin-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;padding-left:4px;font-size:16=
px;margin:0"><span>Furthermore, clinical trials measures do not reflect eff=
ective use of AI in healthcare. One blog about transparency in medicine </s=
pan><strong><a href=3D"https://substack.com/redirect/e67692c4-15c5-4753-b41=
1-baaebfadf1e9?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FA=
k3tf6E" rel=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underline" ta=
rget=3D"_blank">points out</a></strong><span>: =E2=80=9C</span><em>A larger=
 number of trials does not equal more gain in useful scientific knowledge. =
For example, the UK excelled at generating robust and useful evidence on po=
tential Covid treatments precisely because it focused its efforts on a smal=
l number of large, well-designed trials</em><span>.=E2=80=9D</span></p></li=
><li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);line-hei=
