=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underline" target=3D"_bl=
ank">ChinAI #162</a></strong><span>), we should be skeptical of giving too =
much weight to this =E2=80=9CChina leads the world in AI-driven clinical tr=
ials=E2=80=9D indicator. In that ChinAI translation, Leiphone finds that Ch=
ina=E2=80=99s tech giants have struggled to get approvals for their medical=
 AI products. As of 2021, only 15 AI products had successfully passed the c=
ertification process from China=E2=80=99s National Medical Product Administ=
ration. Now, with this context, we can leverage the AI index=E2=80=99s data=
 to present a very different picture: by comparison, the U.S. FDA approved =
129 AI medical devices in 2021 (p. 309).</span></p></li></ul><p style=3D"ma=
rgin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><stron=
g>Second, the index repeats the flawed narrative of an AI optimism gap betw=
een Asian and Western countries: =E2=80=9C</strong><em>A large majority of =
people believe AI-powered products and services offer more benefits than dr=
awbacks in countries like China (83%), Indonesia (80%), and Thailand (77%),=
